lesson: 77
title: More than, fewer than, less than
stars: 0
explain: «4 more than» means the other number and 4 more: if Sam has 9 cards and Lily has 4 more than Sam, Lily has 9 + 4 = 13.
explain: «fewer than» and «less than» mean the other number take away: if Lily has 13 cards and Sam has 4 fewer, Sam has 13 - 4 = 9.
explain: Read carefully who is compared to whom: «Sam has 9, that is 4 fewer than Lily» means Lily has 4 more, 9 + 4 = 13.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing x=6..80 d=2..30
need: x - d >= 2
answer: x
exercise: {b} has {x-d} {t}. {a} has {d} more {t} than {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: {a} has {x+d} {t}. {b} has {d} fewer {t} than {a}. How many {t} does {b} have?
exercise: {a} has {x+d} dollars. {b} has {d} dollars less than {a}. How many dollars does {b} have?
exercise: {b} is {x-d} years old. {a} is {d} years older than {b}. How old is {a}?
exercise: {a} has {x-d} {t}. That is {d} fewer than {b} has. How many {t} does {b} have?
exercise: {a} read {x+d} pages. {b} read {d} pages less than {a}. How many pages did {b} read?
exercise: {b} has {x+d} {t}, which is {d} more than {a} has. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: {b} has {x-d} {t}, and {a} has {d} more than that. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: {a} saved {x+d} dollars, and {b} saved {d} dollars less. How much did {b} save?
test: {b} has only {x-d} {t}; {a} has {d} {t} more. Find how many {t} {a} has.
test: With {x+d} {t}, {a} has {d} {t} more than {b}. How many {t} belong to {b}?
test: {a} counted {x-d} {t}, {d} fewer than {b} counted. How many {t} did {b} count?
exercise: {a} has {d} more {t} than {b}, and {b} has {x-d}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: How many {t} does {b} have if {a} has {x+d} and {b} has {d} fewer?
exercise: In Florida, {a} caught {x-d} fish. {b} caught {d} more fish than {a}. How many fish did {b} catch?
exercise: {a} has {x+d} {t}. {b} does not have as many: {d} {t} fewer. How many {t} does {b} have?
exercise: A red ribbon is {x-d} cm long. A blue ribbon is {d} cm longer. How many cm long is the blue ribbon?
exercise: {b} spent {x+d} dollars in a shop in London. {a} spent {d} dollars less than {b}. How many dollars did {a} spend?
exercise: A school in Sydney has {x-d} computers. Another school has {d} more computers than that. How many computers does the other school have?
exercise: {b} has {x-d} {t}. That is {d} {t} less than {a} has. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: {b} has {x-d} {t}, which is {d} short of what {a} has. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: Find {b}'s number of {t} if {a} owns {x+d} and {b} owns {d} less.
test: {a}'s team in Dublin scored {x+d} points, {d} points more than {b}'s team. How many points did {b}'s team score?
test: A shop in Ontario has {x-d} bikes and {d} more scooters than bikes. How many scooters does the shop have?
test: Compared with {a}, who has {x+d} {t}, {b} has {d} fewer. What is {b}'s number of {t}?
test: {a} walked {x-d} km in a week in New Zealand; {b} walked {d} km further. How many km did {b} walk?
exercise: {b} got {x-d} {t}, {a} got {d} more than that. how many {a} got
exercise: {a} saved {x+d} bucks, {b} saved {d} less. how much {b} got then
exercise: {b}'s {x-d+1}, no sorry {x-d}, n {a}'s {d} years older. how old's {a}
exercise: {a} has {x-d} {t}, thats {d} fewer than {b}. my mate says {b} has {x+d}. nah, how many {b} got really
test: {a}'s team got {x+d} points, {d} more than {b}'s team. {b}'s team got how many
test: red ribbon's {x-d} cm, blue one's {d} cm longer, oh n the scissors cost 4 dollars. how long's the blue one
test: {a} {x+d} {t}, {b} {d} fewer. {a} says {b} has {x+d+d}?? thats wrong innit. so how many {b} got
exam: Whereas {b} holds {x-d} {t} and {a} holds {d} more than {b}, determine the quantity of {t} held by {a}.
exam: It is given that {a} possesses {x+d} {t}, and that {b} possesses {d} fewer than {a}. Ascertain the holding of {b}.
exam: State the number which exceeds {x-d} by {d.words}.
exam: Compute the quantity of {t} held by {b}, given that the holding of {a}, being {x+d}, exceeds that of {b} by {d}.
exercise: When Grandad passed away, he left {b} {x-d} dollars and {a}, the oldest, {d} dollars more. How many dollars did {a} get?
exercise: Last year the factory had {x+d} workers. After the 'restructuring' it has {d} fewer. How many workers does it have now?
test: {b} 'lost' {d} of {a}'s {x+d} {t} — nicked 'em, more like, the sneaky git. how many has {a} got now
